WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.160
Part 2. You will hear some recorded information for visitors to a region

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.840
about history and nature walks there and about a particular visitor attraction.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.840
First, you have some time to look at questions 11 to 14.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:43.040
Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 14.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.720
Here is some information about history and nature walks in the region.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.799
They can be completed in less than a day and also be combined with a visit to Melby,

00:00:56.799 --> 00:01:04.239
a former coal mine now open to the public. The Marsden Coastal Walk is suitable for

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:10.480
all the family as there are no strenuous climbs. The route begins at Marsden Harbour,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:16.480
where there are hourly ferries to and from the beautiful Hooker Island, a great place for fishing.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.599
The walk covers part of the Coastal Path Trail. As there are clear signposts all along the way,

00:01:23.599 --> 00:01:30.000
you don't even need to take a map with you. At one point, the route goes inland slightly

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.519
and passes a castle built in the 1400s, near to a now-vanished market town.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.519
The castle's now just a pile of stones, but it's a great place to take photos,

00:01:41.519 --> 00:01:45.040
and fascinating information boards show what it once looked like.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.040
If you set off early, you can be back in time for midday.

00:01:49.919 --> 00:01:55.680
The Melby Heritage Walk is a great place to take photos, especially of the night sky.

00:01:56.559 --> 00:02:00.639
At certain times of the year, people come here from far and wide.

00:02:01.279 --> 00:02:04.959
They climb to the top of the valley and take pictures of the stars.

00:02:05.919 --> 00:02:12.160
In the daytime, it's completely different. As you hike through the dense woods in the valley bottom,

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:15.919
the only things you'll hear are the sound of your own footsteps.

00:02:16.399 --> 00:02:21.679
At the highest point, you can stop to take in the views, and those with lots of energy

00:02:21.679 --> 00:02:27.759
can climb the tower that's situated where there was once a 17th-century hunting lodge.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.759
The route continues along the tops of the hills,

00:02:31.759 --> 00:02:36.639
and brings you back down to the starting point, the car park at Melby Coalmine.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.679
Before you hear the rest of the recorded information,

00:02:43.679 --> 00:02:47.759
you have some time to look at questions 15 to 20.

00:03:14.479 --> 00:03:18.320
Melby Coalmine has been open to the public for 20 years,

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.880
and has won awards for its visitor experience.

00:03:21.919 --> 00:03:24.880
Many of the buildings around the mine are still standing,

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.600
and have been converted into display areas.

00:03:28.639 --> 00:03:32.960
Firstly, there's an exhibition showing the history of Melby Coalmine.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.039
The exhibition is about the history of Melby Coalmine,

00:03:37.039 --> 00:03:45.199
Firstly, there's an exhibition showing the history of the mine,

00:03:45.199 --> 00:03:47.600
with many original black-and-white photos.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:54.080
To see that, you go from the car park via a covered walkway to the main visitor centre.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.919
Go through the ticket office to an area where there are lockers to leave heavy bags in,

00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:01.919
and where you can borrow raincoats.

00:04:02.639 --> 00:04:05.039
Beyond that room is the exhibition.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.880
There's a small bathhouse where miners used to wash after their shift underground.

00:04:11.679 --> 00:04:15.839
You can see that in the building directly to the north of the engine house.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.600
The boiler has gone from there now, but there are lines of tin baths on the stone floor.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.279
There's a display of early mining tools from the days before mechanisation.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:33.519
You can find that in a small L-shaped building in the middle of the northern boundary of the site.

00:04:34.239 --> 00:04:40.160
It's incredible to think how miners were able to use hand implements to cut through rock.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.519
The vehicle shed, where you can find wagons of different sizes,

00:04:45.519 --> 00:04:50.720
along with some of the high-tech cutting machines that were in use until the mine closed,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.160
is in the southwest corner of the site and can be accessed via a covered walkway.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.920
There is a field with ponies, which are always popular with children,

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.559
on the north-eastern boundary of the site, not approached via a covered walkway.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.040
The other building to mention is the education centre.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.799
This is where school groups go when they visit the mine,

00:05:14.799 --> 00:05:20.480
but it's also accessible to the public, as it contains a library and small gift shop.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.559
The centre is connected to the ticket office via a short section of covered walkway.

00:05:26.559 --> 00:05:40.399
That is the end of part 2. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part 2.

